WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.470
Environment-controlled paths can influence
where programs look for files,

2
00:00:04.550 --> 00:00:09.009
tools, plugins, libraries, configuration,
temporary storage, and helper programs.

3
00:00:09.089 --> 00:00:11.166
This is useful for flexible deployment,

4
00:00:11.246 --> 00:00:15.461
but it becomes risky when sensitive
operations depend on ambient search behavior

5
00:00:15.541 --> 00:00:17.679
rather than explicit trusted locations.

6
00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:21.363
For security-sensitive operations, prefer
explicit trusted paths.

7
00:00:21.443 --> 00:00:25.352
If a program must open a specific
configuration file, launch a known helper,

8
00:00:25.432 --> 00:00:26.898
or load a trusted component,

9
00:00:26.978 --> 00:00:31.254
the location should come from controlled
configuration or a trusted installation

10
00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:35.304
path, not from a broad search process
shaped by unrelated environment state.

11
00:00:35.384 --> 00:00:38.011
Executable lookup behavior should be
deliberate.

12
00:00:38.091 --> 00:00:42.489
A child process launch that relies on
ambient search settings can behave differently

13
00:00:42.569 --> 00:00:46.295
depending on who started the program and
what environment it inherited.

14
00:00:46.375 --> 00:00:50.589
Sensitive launch code should specify the
intended program clearly and define the

15
00:00:50.669 --> 00:00:52.624
environment that the child receives.

16
00:00:52.704 --> 00:00:56.980
If the application accepts search paths,
they should be constrained, documented,

17
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:57.854
and validated.

18
00:00:57.934 --> 00:01:00.805
Each entry should be checked for expected
form, length,

19
00:01:00.885 --> 00:01:04.061
allowed roots where appropriate, and
empty-entry behavior.

20
00:01:04.141 --> 00:01:08.295
The program should also define ordering
and what happens when a path is unusable.

21
00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:12.895
Environment-derived file paths should pass
the same validation and permission checks

22
00:01:12.975 --> 00:01:14.257
as other external paths.

23
00:01:14.337 --> 00:01:18.064
Review should trace any environment
variable that affects file access,

24
00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:22.114
tool launch, loading behavior, plugin
discovery, or command construction.

25
00:01:22.194 --> 00:01:25.492
The question is not whether the value came
from the environment;

26
00:01:25.572 --> 00:01:28.260
it is what authority the program gives to
that value.
